@mcollina Yep I'll work on a PR. In my mind, I'm debating whether this belongs as a global configuration (on the Fastify constructor logger options), or as an encapsulated hook (like `onRequest`) which can be encapsulated on a per-plugin (or per-route) basis.

For the use case I personally have in mind, a global configuration will be fine because it depends only on the platform that the app runs on. However I guess it is conceivable that different plugins/routes may receive requests from different platforms which may need to parse bindings in different ways? Or maybe that's overcomplicating it.

Thoughts?

Ok sounds fair to me. Your comment raised another question: should this be

1. a hook to modify the bindings object before being passed to `logger.child()` internally (this is what I initially thought) e.g.
```Javascript
fastify({
  ...
  bindChildLogger: (bindings, loggerOpts, req) => {
    bindings.myKey = ... // calculate something base on req
    bindings.reqId // do something to the base bindings (request ID)
  }
})
```
2. a hook to create the child logger itself (e.g. take the base bindings and return a new logger instance) as alluded by your "logger factory" name
```Javascript
fastify({
  ...
  childLoggerFactory: (logger, bindings, loggerOpts, req) => {
    bindings.myKey = ... // calculate something base on req
    return logger.child(bindings, loggerOpts)
  }
})
```

We are already able to pass our own logger implementation anyway (so we can have our own `logger.child()` implementation), however we do not have access to the request object from there. The second option is untimately more flexible I guess.
